  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale: We implement a genetic algorithm (GA) to explore the solution space for improved upper bounds on the maximum overlap integral. GAs are suitable for this non-convex optimization problem due to their ability to escape local optima and handle constraints effectively. The core idea is to evolve a population of candidate solutions, each representing a step function h, by applying crossover and mutation operators while ensuring the sum constraint (\u2211h[i] = n_points/2). The initial solution (n=95 samples) is incorporated as the initial population to guide the search. The algorithm's bottleneck is computational time per fitness evaluation (involving cross-correlation), so we optimize GA parameters to minimize generations while respecting the 1100-second budget.  \n\n2. Mathematical Specification:  \n   - **Variables**: Each candidate is a vector h of length n_points (95) with h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2.  \n   - **Objective**: Minimize C_5 = (2.0/n_points) * max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')).  \n   - **Constraints**: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2.  \n   - **Fitness**: Derived from the objective, lower values are better.  \n\n3. Implementation Procedure:  \n   a. Initialization: Generate an initial population. If a parent solution (initial_h_values) is provided, use it. Otherwise, generate random feasible vectors (ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 via clipping and scaling).  \n   b. Fitness Evaluation: Repair any infeasible candidates by clipping values to [0,1] and scaling to satisfy the sum constraint. Compute the crossing correlation and C_5.  \n   c. Selection: Tournament selection with tournament size 3.  \n   d. Crossover: Single-point crossover with 70% probability.  \n   e. Mutation: Apply Gaussian mutation to each gene with 1% probability per gene, standard deviation 0.1. Repair mutated candidates.  \n   f. Elitism: Retain the best candidate from the previous generation.  \n   g. Termination: Stop when the budget is exceeded or a maximum generation count (200) is reached.  \n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:  \n   - Fixed n_points = 95.  \n   - Tournament selection with size 3 ensures diversity.  \n   - Mutation rate (1%) and standard deviation (0.1) are chosen to balance exploration and intensity.  \n   - GA parameters are fixed; variations (e.g., crossover rate, mutation probability) would be costly to compute within the budget and are not explored.  \n\n5. Numerical Robustness and Memory Use:  \n   - Repair is performed after crossover and mutation to ensure feasibility.  \n   - Feasible candidates are explicitly stored; infeasible ones are discarded.  \n   - Use a fixed population size (200) to balance memory and computation.  \n\n6. Runtime Safeguards and Return Contract:  \n   - Tracks elapsed time to enforce the budget constraint.  \n   - Returns the best candidate (lowest C_5) with its score and n_points=95.  \n   - Error handling: Ensures no division-by-zero in repair and handles simulation termination gracefully.",
